---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * The Chrome Web Store requires a privacy policy URL, and this is it. The
 * store also makes the publisher certify that the policy matches the
 * disclosures on the listing — so the claims here are generated from
 * src/data/legal.ts, which is the same file the listing copy is checked
 * against. They cannot drift apart without someone editing the shared source.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import {
  dataProtection,
  extensionDataFacts,
  extensionVersions,
  legal,
  licenceSync,
  security,
  updatedLabel,
} from "../../data/legal";
import { CONSENT_CATEGORIES } from "../../data/consent";
import { toddleExtension } from "../../data/site";

const title = "Privacy policy — Nyuchi Learning";
const description =
  "What the Toddle Enhancement Extension and learning.nyuchi.com do with data. In short: nothing the extension reads leaves your browser — it sends only feedback you choose to send and, while a licence is entered, a daily check for cancelled keys that carries no data; the website asks before it measures anything, and lets you take all, some or none of it.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="/legal/privacy">
  <article class="container-prose section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-h1 text-foreground text-balance">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Last updated {updatedLabel}. Applies to
      <strong class="text-foreground">learning.nyuchi.com</strong> and to the
      <strong class="text-foreground">{toddleExtension.name}</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 ubuntu-quote">
      <p class="text-body text-pretty">
        <strong>The short version.</strong> The extension reads Toddle inside your own
        browser, for you at the screen, and sends it nowhere. There is no account, no
        analytics and no tracking, and your licence key is checked on your own machine. The
        extension makes only two connections outside Toddle: feedback you write in its menu
        and choose to send, and, while a licence is entered, a once-a-day download of a
        signed list of cancelled keys that sends nothing about you. Neither ever includes
        anything from Toddle. {licenceSync.short} We do hold your email address if you bought something, the
        way any shop does. This website asks before it measures anything and lets you take
        all, some or none of it; the extension measures nothing at all, and never will.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Who we are</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      {legal.entity}, trading as Nyuchi Learning, registered in {legal.country}; the site
      and the extension are run by its development division, {legal.operator}, in
      {legal.city}. For anything in this policy, write to
      <a
        href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}
        class="link"
        >{legal.privacyEmail}</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">The extension</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      Each of the following is a statement about how the software is built, not a promise
      about how we intend to behave. They can be checked against the extension itself.
    </p>

    <dl class="mt-6 space-y-6">
      {
        extensionDataFacts.map((fact) => (
          <div class="card p-6">
            <dt class="font-serif text-h5 text-foreground">{fact.claim}</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">{fact.detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h2 id="extension-feedback" class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      The extension's feedback form
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      This is one of the extension's two connections outside Toddle (the other is the
      <a class="link" href="#revocation-check">daily check for cancelled keys</a>), and it
      only happens when you make it happen. Since version 0.8.1 the extension's toolbar menu has a
      <strong class="text-foreground">Send feedback</strong> form.
    </p>
    <ul
      class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-body text-muted-foreground list-disc list-outside pl-5 marker:text-foreground/40"
    >
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">When.</strong> Only when you press Send. Opening
        the form, typing in it or closing it sends nothing, and nothing is ever sent in the
        background.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">What.</strong> Exactly what you typed — the topic,
        your message, and your email address only if you enter one — plus the extension's
        version number, so we know which release you mean.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">What it never includes.</strong> Nothing from
        Toddle: no student data, no page address, no class or school details, and not your
        licence key. Please leave student names out of what you type, too.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">Where it goes.</strong> To Nyuchi's feedback form,
        which is processed by Formspree, a form service acting on our behalf. It is the
        same service and the same form as this website's
        <a class="link" href="/feedback">feedback page</a>, and it is handled the same way
        once it reaches us.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">Why.</strong> To read what you tell us, reply if
        you asked us to, and decide what to fix or build next. We do not add you to a
        mailing list or share it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">How long.</strong> Feedback is kept only as long as
        we need it to act on it. You can ask for yours to be deleted at any time by writing
        to
        <a class="link" href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="revocation-check" class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      The daily check for cancelled keys
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      The extension's second connection outside Toddle, from version 0.8.2.
    </p>
    <ul
      class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-body text-muted-foreground list-disc list-outside pl-5 marker:text-foreground/40"
    >
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">When.</strong> At most once a day, and only while a
        licence key is entered; a key just entered is checked within 5 minutes. Without a
        licence it never happens.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">What is sent.</strong> One plain request to
        <code class="break-all">https://licenses.nyuchi.com/v1/revocations</code>, from the extension's
        background worker (version {extensionVersions.previous} and earlier asked
        <code>licences.nyuchi.dev</code>, the same server). It carries no licence key, no identifiers, no cookies and no
        Toddle data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">What comes back.</strong> A list, signed by Nyuchi,
        of the fingerprints (SHA-256 hashes) of cancelled keys. The extension checks its own
        key against the list on your machine, and keeps only the result.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">Who sees what.</strong> Cloudflare, which runs the
        server, sees your IP address, as with any web request. Nyuchi does not log it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">If it fails.</strong> The last result stands, so a
        network outage never switches a licence off.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="security" class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      Security
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      The statements above are enforced in the software and held in place by tests that
      fail if the extension asks for a new permission, sends anything anywhere it is not
      meant to, or sends a query that is not on its fixed, read-only list. It has had two
      adversarial reviews. Every finding of the first, on {security.reviews[0].label}, was
      fixed in version {security.reviews[0].version}; every finding of the second, on {security.reviews[1].label},
      of the code for version {security.reviews[1].version}, a test release, was fixed in
      that release, and the fixes are in version {security.version}, on the Chrome Web
      Store.
      The <a class="link" href="/legal/security">Security page</a> sets out the security model, how it is tested, what the reviews found, the limits
      no extension can remove, and how to report a vulnerability.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">
      Are you a processor of our students' data?
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      No. A data processor receives personal data and does something with it on the
      controller's behalf. We receive none: the extension reads what your browser has
      already loaded from Toddle, renders it differently on that same screen, and
      transmits none of it. Feedback a teacher chooses to send from the extension's menu
      carries only what they typed, never anything from Toddle, and the daily licence check
      sends nothing at all. Your school remains the controller of its data, and Toddle
      remains its processor, exactly as before the extension was installed.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If your data protection officer would like that in writing against a specific
      framework, or wants to review the code before approving it, write to us — we would
      rather answer the question properly than have you take a web page's word for it. A
      <a class="link" href="/legal/data#dpa">data processing agreement</a> is available on
      request.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">This website</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      It is a set of static pages served by Vercel. Vercel processes the request in order
      to serve it and keeps operational logs, as any host must.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If you use the <a class="link" href="/feedback">feedback form</a> on this website,
      what you type —
      the topic, your message, your email address if you give one, and the page you came
      from — is sent through Formspree, a form service, and recorded as an issue in
      {legal.shortEntity}'s private GitHub repository for the extension, where only our
      team can see it. We use it to reply to you and to decide what to build; we do not
      add you to a mailing list or share it. Feedback is kept only as long as we need it to
      act on it; ask us at
      <a class="link" href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a> and
      we will delete it sooner. Feedback sent from inside the extension goes to the same
      form — see <a class="link" href="#extension-feedback">the extension's feedback form</a>.
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Your choices, and what they control</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      The first time you visit, a banner asks. Nothing optional is switched on until you
      switch it on, and you can take all of it, some of it, or none. Ignoring the banner
      leaves you exactly where refusing does.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5">
      {
        CONSENT_CATEGORIES.map((category) => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-serif text-h4 text-foreground">
              {category.name}
              {category.required && (
                <span class="text-body-sm text-muted-foreground font-sans"> · always on</span>
              )}
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
              {category.detail}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-body-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
              {category.used}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground">Changing your mind costs one press.</strong>
      <strong class="text-foreground">Cookie choices</strong> at the bottom of any page reopens
      the panel with your current answers, and withdrawing is as easy as agreeing was. We keep
      a record of what you chose and when, because the law puts the burden of showing that on
      us rather than on you — it is stored in your own browser, alongside the choice itself.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      Every cookie and stored value, with what sets it and how long it lasts, is listed in
      the <a class="link" href="/legal/cookies">cookie policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      This is written to meet the two laws we hold ourselves to (see
      <a class="link" href="#law">the law we follow</a>): Zimbabwe's
      <strong class="text-foreground">{dataProtection.home.law}</strong>, and the
      <strong class="text-foreground">EU and UK GDPR</strong>. They agree on the substance,
      so one approach serves both: nothing optional before you ask for it, each purpose
      chosen separately, refusal as easy as agreement, withdrawal at any time, and a record
      we can produce.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      One detail we would rather state than have you discover. While consent is denied,
      Google's tag is still on the page and still contacts Google, without cookies and
      without identifying you — that is how Google's consent mechanism is built. So "no
      cookies until you agree" is exactly true; "no contact with Google until you agree"
      would not be, and we are not going to claim it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground"
        >None of that is true of the extension.</strong
      > It contains no analytics, and nothing it reads leaves your browser; its only outside
      connections are the feedback form, when you press Send, and the daily licence check
      described above. This is the website measuring
      the website. If the distinction matters to you — and for anyone assessing the
      software for a school, it is the part that matters — the extension section above is
      the one to read.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      Fonts are loaded from Google Fonts, so your browser fetches them from Google and
      Google sees your IP address in doing so.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground">The support messenger is opt-in.</strong> There is a
      Support button in the corner of every page. Until you press it Intercom loads nothing
      and sets no cookie of its own — the analytics above is separate, and arrives without
      being asked. Press it and the messenger loads from
      <a href={legal.helpCentre} rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">Intercom</a>, which
      at that point sets its own cookies, can see which pages you have open while you use
      it, and holds the conversation on our behalf so we can answer you. We chose to load it
      on demand rather than on arrival precisely so that reading this site costs you
      nothing.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">If you buy a licence</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      This is the one place we hold personal data about you, and it is worth being exact
      about it, because it is easy to confuse with what the extension does.
    </p>
    <ul
      class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-body text-muted-foreground list-disc list-outside pl-5 marker:text-foreground/40"
    >
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">We hold your email address and your order.</strong>
        We need them to issue your key, to let you renew, and to give you an invoice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">Your payment goes to Buy Me a Coffee, not to us.</strong>
        It processes the order, with Stripe handling the card, and passes us your email
        address and what you bought so we can issue your key. We never see your card
        details.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">Your key contains your email.</strong> A key may also
        name who it is for: your email address, or for an organisation licence the school's
        email domain. Where it does, the extension compares that with the Toddle account
        signed in, inside your browser only. The key is stored in your own browser and
        checked there — the extension does not send it to us or to anyone else, not even in
        the daily check for cancelled keys.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">We do not market to you off the back of it.</strong>
        Buying a licence does not put you on a mailing list.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      We keep order records for as long as tax law requires us to, and no longer for any
      other purpose.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Email</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If you email us we keep the email, because that is what email is. We use it to
      answer you and to keep a record of support requests. Support runs on
      <a href={legal.helpCentre} rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">Intercom</a>, so
      Intercom processes those conversations on our behalf. We do not add you to a
      marketing list off the back of a support question.
    </p>

    <h2 id="controller" class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      Who is responsible, and on what basis
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      For the personal data Nyuchi holds — licence purchases, feedback, support
      conversations and this website's analytics — {legal.entity} is the data controller,
      under {legal.country}'s {dataProtection.home.law} and under the EU and UK GDPR. Our
      legal basis for each use is:
    </p>
    <ul
      class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-body text-muted-foreground list-disc list-outside pl-5 marker:text-foreground/40"
    >
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">your consent</strong> for this website's analytics
        and the support messenger, which you can withdraw at any time (GDPR Article
        6(1)(a));
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">the contract with you</strong> to issue, deliver and
        support a licence you buy (Article 6(1)(b)), and
        <strong class="text-foreground">the law</strong> for the records a business must
        keep (Article 6(1)(c));
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">your request</strong> when you send feedback or write
        to us: our legitimate interest in reading and answering what you choose to send
        (Article 6(1)(f)). You can ask us to delete it at any time.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      For student data in Toddle, the school is the controller; the extension's processing
      happens on the school's own devices and Nyuchi receives none of it. The
      <a class="link" href="/legal/data">data handling</a> page lists what we hold, the
      services that process it for us, and how long it is kept, and
      <a class="link" href="/legal/student-privacy">student privacy</a> covers FERPA, COPPA
      and data processing agreements.
    </p>

    <h2 id="law" class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      The law we follow
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground">Zimbabwe's {dataProtection.home.law}</strong> applies
      to us because {legal.entity} is a Zimbabwean company. Its data protection authority is
      <a href={dataProtection.home.authorityUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link"
        >{dataProtection.home.authority}</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground">The EU and UK GDPR</strong> are the most widely used
      standard for personal data, so we apply them to everyone, wherever you live, not only
      to people in Europe. Where the two laws differ, we follow the stricter one.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground">Where your data goes.</strong> Nyuchi is in
      {legal.country}, and the services that process data for us (listed on the
      <a class="link" href="/legal/data">data handling</a> page) are mostly in the United
      States. So data you send us may be processed outside your country. {legal.country}
      does not have an adequacy decision from the EU or the UK, so for data that comes to us
      from there we rely on the safeguards each service provides for international
      transfers, such as standard contractual clauses in its data processing terms. Ask us
      and we will tell you which applies to each service. None of this involves student data
      from the extension, which does not leave the teacher's browser.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rights" class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      Your rights
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      Whatever country you are in, you can ask us to:
    </p>
    <ul
      class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-body text-muted-foreground list-disc list-outside pl-5 marker:text-foreground/40"
    >
      <li>tell you what personal data we hold about you, and give you a copy;</li>
      <li>correct it if it is wrong or incomplete;</li>
      <li>delete it, unless the law requires us to keep it;</li>
      <li>stop or limit how we use it while a question about it is settled;</li>
      <li>give it to you, or to someone you choose, in a common machine-readable format;</li>
      <li>stop using it where we rely on our legitimate interests;</li>
      <li>
        withdraw your consent at any time, without affecting what was done before you
        withdrew it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      We make no decisions about you by automated means alone, and we do not profile you.
      Write to
      <a
        href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}
        class="link"
        >{legal.privacyEmail}</a
      >. There is no charge, and we answer within {dataProtection.gdpr.answerWithin}.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground">You can complain to a regulator</strong>, though we
      would like the chance to put it right first. In Zimbabwe, that is
      <a href={dataProtection.home.authorityUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link"
        >{dataProtection.home.authorityShort}</a
      >. In the UK, it is
      <a href={dataProtection.gdpr.ukAuthorityUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link"
        >{dataProtection.gdpr.ukAuthority}</a
      >. In the EU and EEA, it is the
      <a href={dataProtection.gdpr.euAuthoritiesUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link"
        >data protection authority</a
      > where you live, work, or where you think the problem happened.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">If something goes wrong</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If a breach affects personal data we hold, we will tell
      {dataProtection.home.authorityShort} within {dataProtection.home.breachHours} hours of
      finding it, as Zimbabwe's Act requires; tell the EU or UK regulator within
      {dataProtection.gdpr.breachHours} hours where the GDPR requires it; and tell the
      people and schools affected without undue delay. The
      <a class="link" href="/legal/data#breach">data handling</a> page says what we will tell
      you. The extension holds no student data, so a breach on our side cannot expose
      student data from Toddle.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Changes</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If what the software does with data changes, this page changes with it and the date
      at the top moves. A change that widened what we collect would be announced, not
      slipped in.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
